From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:04:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] iio: qcom_smgr: stream at the sampling frequency userspace
 set

sampling_frequency is writable, but the value was never used: every
buffer enable picked the fastest native rate again, and a buffer disable
set the stored rate to 0, so the attribute read 0 until the next enable.
SMGR sends one QMI indication for nearly every sample, so a sensor kept
streaming at its top rate wakes the application processor that often even
when the reader only wants a few samples a second (iio-sensor-proxy reads
the accelerometer for screen rotation every 700 ms).

Keep the requested rate across buffer disables, use it at buffer enable,
and start from the same default as before: the fastest native rate,
capped at the SMGR maximum. Rates under 1 Hz are rejected. Rates above
the maximum are capped to it rather than rejected, so that such a write
keeps working: iio-sensor-proxy writes 10 Hz to a sensor that reads under
10 Hz when it opens it, and the light sensor's maximum is 5 Hz. Fractions
are dropped, as SMGR takes whole hertz.

The attribute reads back the requested rate. What SMGR then streams at
can differ: it uses the sensor's native rates, the accelerometer's lowest
being about 12.5 Hz.

Only the result of the buffering response is checked, as before: its
ack_nak field is 1 for the light sensor, which streams normally.

The driver is in the msm8996-mainline tree (v6.19.5-msm8996), not in
mainline yet.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (SM-T820), counting samples over six seconds:
the accelerometer at 12, 50, 100, 200 and 416 Hz streams at 12.2, 49.3,
98.6, 197 and 394 Hz, the gyroscope at 10, 100 and 416 Hz at 12.2, 99 and
396 Hz, the magnetometer at 10 and 50 Hz at 10.1 and 50.0 Hz. Writing
10 Hz to the light sensor reads back 5 and streams at 4.8 Hz, and
iio-sensor-proxy claims and reads it with no warning.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 50 ++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index e3bc6c272ddd..a88a9f554572 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -159,6 +159,24 @@ static int qcom_smgr_request_single_sensor_info(struct qcom_smgr *smgr,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The sample rate to stream at until userspace sets another: the fastest
+ * native rate of the sensor, capped at the maximum SMGR supports for it,
+ * which may be lower.
+ */
+static u16 qcom_smgr_default_sample_rate(const struct qcom_smgr_data_type_item *dt)
+{
+	u16 rate = 0;
+
+	if (dt->native_sample_rates)
+		rate = dt->native_sample_rates[dt->native_sample_rate_count - 1];
+
+	if (rate == 0 || rate > dt->max_sample_rate)
+		rate = dt->max_sample_rate;
+
+	return rate;
+}
+
 static int qcom_smgr_request_buffering(struct qcom_smgr *smgr,
 				       struct qcom_smgr_sensor *sensor,
 				       bool enable)
@@ -189,20 +207,14 @@ static int qcom_smgr_request_buffering(struct qcom_smgr *smgr,
 		 * A report cannot have 0 samples, therefore report rate cannot
 		 * be higher than sample rate.
 		 *
-		 * Fow now we default to the maximum sample rate and set the
-		 * report rate such that every report contains only 1 sample.
-		 * This gives us the lowest latency.
-		 */
-		if (dt->native_sample_rates)
-			sample_rate = dt->native_sample_rates
-					[dt->native_sample_rate_count - 1];
-
-		/*
-		 * SMGR may support a lower maximum sample rate than natively
-		 * supported by the sensor.
+		 * Stream at the sampling frequency set through sysfs (by
+		 * default the fastest one) and set the report rate to match,
+		 * so that a report usually contains 1 sample. This gives us
+		 * the lowest latency.
 		 */
+		sample_rate = dt->cur_sample_rate;
 		if (sample_rate == 0 || sample_rate > dt->max_sample_rate)
-			sample_rate = dt->max_sample_rate;
+			sample_rate = qcom_smgr_default_sample_rate(dt);
 
 		req.report_rate = sample_rate * SMGR_REPORT_RATE_HZ;
 
@@ -252,7 +264,8 @@ static int qcom_smgr_request_buffering(struct qcom_smgr *smgr,
 	}
 
 	/* Keep track of requested sample rate */
-	dt->cur_sample_rate = sample_rate;
+	if (enable)
+		dt->cur_sample_rate = sample_rate;
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -440,13 +453,19 @@ static int qcom_smgr_iio_write_raw(struct iio_dev *iio_dev,
 				   int val2, long mask)
 {
 	struct qcom_smgr_iio_priv *priv = iio_priv(iio_dev);
+	struct qcom_smgr_data_type_item *dt =
+		&priv->sensor->data_types[priv->sensor->data_type];
 
 	switch (mask) {
 	case IIO_CHAN_INFO_SAMP_FREQ:
+		if (val < 1)
+			return -EINVAL;
+
 		if (!iio_device_claim_direct(iio_dev))
 			return -EBUSY;
 
-		priv->sensor->data_types[priv->sensor->data_type].cur_sample_rate = val;
+		/* SMGR takes whole hertz, up to the maximum it reports */
+		dt->cur_sample_rate = min_t(int, val, dt->max_sample_rate);
 
 		iio_device_release_direct(iio_dev);
 		return 0;
@@ -894,9 +913,8 @@ static int qcom_smgr_probe(struct qrtr_device *qdev)
 				smgr->sensors[i].id);
 
 		for (j = 0; j < smgr->sensors[i].data_type_count; j++) {
-			/* Default to maximum sample rate */
 			smgr->sensors[i].data_types[j].cur_sample_rate =
-				smgr->sensors[i].data_types[j].max_sample_rate;
+				qcom_smgr_default_sample_rate(&smgr->sensors[i].data_types[j]);
 
 			dev_dbg(smgr->dev, "0x%02x,%d: type %u, %s %s, max %u Hz, range %u\n",
 				smgr->sensors[i].id, j,
-- 
2.56.0

